Right elbow pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Carolyn is a 49-year-old female who presents for follow-up of chronic problems. She reports that her osteoarthritis has been flaring up lately, primarily in her elbows. She notes that she has been sitting at the desk for long periods at work, which she thinks may have increased the flare-ups. She denies any other joint pain. She notes that she is right-handed and she types more than writes. She denies any numbness or tingling in her hands. She also denies any other issues with gout, as her last episode was 3 months ago and she has been doing well on the medication.

Ms. is compliant with the clobetasol for her scalp psoriasis and it has been under control. She reports that she has not had any flare-ups of psoriasis since the last episode.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Musculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain.
• Neurological: Denies numbness or tingling in the hands.
• Skin: Reports psoriasis under control.

PHYSICAL EXAMINATION

• Respiratory: Clear to auscultation bilaterally.
• Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm.
• Gastrointestinal: Soft, non-tender abdomen.
• Musculoskeletal: Right elbow shows edema and inflammation of the olecranon. There is tenderness and an effusion of the right olecranon. Pain to palpation of the olecranon bursa. Pain with pronation and supination. Pain with flexion and extension of the right arm. No lower extremity edema.

RESULTS

• Autoimmune panel: Within normal limits.
• Erythrocyte sedimentation rate: Within normal limits.
• Uric acid: Within normal limits.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

• Gout: No signs of gout at this time. We will continue her on colchicine 0.6 mg twice a day as needed for flare-ups.
• Osteoarthritis: Right elbow pain. We will order a right elbow x-ray. We will refer for physical therapy after the x-ray results. We will continue her on meloxicam 15 mg once a day for pain and swelling.
• Psoriasis: Stable. We will continue her on clobetasol cream for her scalp.

