Right elbow pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Anna Smith is a 26-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right elbow pain. The patient states that she is a 26-year-old female with a past medical history significant for epilepsy, depression, and peptic ulcer disease who presents with joint pain. The patient reports that she has been experiencing pain in her left elbow, specifically on the tip, which is stiff and hurts. She notes that she sits at her desk all day for work and types, which exacerbates the pain. She denies any swelling. The patient is right-handed. She denies any numbness or tingling in her hands. She reports some weakness in her arm, but this is not significant. She denies any fever or chills. The patient is still taking her Keppra and has not had any recent seizures. She reports that she has been doing well with her therapy and is currently not taking medication for depression. She denies any feelings of wanting to hurt herself or someone else. She has been doing well with her peptic ulcer disease and has not had any issues since her endoscopy.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Constitutional: Denies fever or chills.
• Musculoskeletal: Endorses left elbow pain.
• Neurological: Denies numbness or tingling.
• Respiratory: Endorses nasal congestion.
• Gastrointestinal: Endorses a little bit of nasal congestion from allergies.

VITALS

All vital signs are within normal limits.

PHYSICAL EXAM

MSK
- Examination of the left elbow reveals erythema and edema of the left elbow. Palpation of the olecranon is painful. The patient reports pain with flexion and extension of the left elbow. There is a palpable left radial pulse.

RESULTS

• X-ray of the left arm: No evidence of any elbow fracture.

• Endoscopy results: Gastritis.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

• Right elbow pain.
- Medical Reasoning: The patient presents with pain in her left elbow, specifically on the tip, which is stiff and hurts. She reports that she sits at her desk all day for work and types, which exacerbates the pain. She denies any swelling. She is right-handed. She denies any numbness or tingling in her hands. She reports some weakness in her arm, but this is not significant. She denies any fever or chills.
- Medical Treatment: The patient will get a little pillow for her elbow. She will be advised to get some rest there. I will prescribe Meloxicam 15 mg once a day.

• Epilepsy.
- Medical Reasoning: The patient is doing well. She has been taking Keppra 500 mg twice a day. She has not had any recent seizures.
- Medical Treatment: Continue Keppra 500 mg twice a day. Refill prescription.

• Depression.
- Medical Reasoning: The patient is doing well with her therapy. She is currently not taking medication.
- Medical Treatment: We can start her on Lexapro 10 mg once a day. We can discuss medical treatment options if she prefers.

• Peptic Ulcer Disease.
- Medical Reasoning: The patient is doing well with her peptic ulcer disease. She has not had any issues since her endoscopy.
- Medical Treatment: Continue Protonix 40 mg once a day before meals.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up in 2 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>
