summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

High blood pressure.

SOCIAL HISTORY

Patient reports that he has been in the emergency room with high blood pressure and palpitations. He states that he was drinking a few cups of coffee without breakfast and thinks it was a panic attack.

FAMILY HISTORY

Patient reports that his family has a high incidence of high blood pressure.

MEDICATIONS

Patient reports that he is on Benicar HCT 40/25 mg daily and Amlodipine 10 mg daily. He is still taking these medications.

ALLERGIES

None reported.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain, palpitations.
• Respiratory: Denies dyspnea on exertion.
• Gastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting.
• Musculoskeletal: Denies muscle pain, joint pain.
• Neurological: Denies headaches, dizziness, seizures.
• Genitourinary: Denies dysuria, hematuria.

VITALS

• BP: 146/75 mmHg.

PHYSICAL EXAM

• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally, no wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.
• Cardiovascular: No murmurs, gallops, or rubs.
• Musculoskeletal: No edema.

RESULTS

• Thyroid profile from March 1, 2021: Thyroid-stimulating hormone 2.4 mcg/mL.
• Potassium: 4.0 mmol/L.

ASSESSMENT

• Resistant hypertension.
• Hypertension.
• Palpitations.

PLAN

Patient is currently on three medications at maximum doses. He will add Spironolactone 25 mg daily. We will monitor his potassium and sodium levels in 2 weeks. He will have a metabolic panel and blood pressure check in 2 weeks. He was advised that the next medication would be carvedilol, which he was advised about the potential side effects. We discussed the potential side effects of spironolactone, including gynecomastia and electrolyte disturbances. He was advised to hydrate and watch for signs of dehydration. He was also advised that if he is not controlled on spironolactone, we will refer him to the resistant hypertension clinic. }}<|eot_id|>
