summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Irritable Bowel Syndrome.

MEDICAL HISTORY

The patient has a history of Irritable Bowel Syndrome (IBS) and Functional Abdominal Pain.

SOCIAL HISTORY

The patient reports that she goes out to eat on Friday and Saturday nights and has been eating spicy foods or spicy shrimp. She has also been drinking with her friends.

FAMILY HISTORY

The patient reports that she has a family history of gastrointestinal problems.

MEDICATIONS

The patient was prescribed Bentyl 20 mg as needed for intestinal cramping. She was also prescribed Reglan 10 mg as needed for nausea, but she has not been using it. She was prescribed Zofran as needed for nausea.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Constitutional: Reports flares of abdominal pain.
Gastrointestinal: Reports diarrhea, nausea, and abdominal pain.
Musculoskeletal: Reports abdominal pain.
Psychiatric: Reports stress.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Constitutional: Alert and cooperative, stated age.
Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.
Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm.
Gastrointestinal: Soft, non-tender abdomen, no masses or organomegaly, normal active bowel sounds.

RESULTS

None.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Brittany Edwards is a pleasant 32-year-old female who was last seen in August of 2019 with flares of abdominal pain, who was diagnosed with Irritable Bowel Syndrome. She was previously prescribed Reglan that she received from the emergency room that she used as needed for nausea. She was instructed to start a low dose fiber supplement, such as Citrucel, daily, probiotics to help with gas and bloating, Bentyl up to four times daily for intestinal cramping, and to stop Reglan and use Zofran as needed. Neuromodulators such as Elavil and Buspar were discussed but not started.

The patient reports that she has been going out to eat some on Friday nights and Saturday nights and that she has had some spicy foods or spicy shrimp. She reports that she is still having the cramping and sharp pains, so she has been avoiding heavy foods. She did have some fried chicken today, though.

The patient reports that she is having bowel movements between 2 and 3 times a day. They are formed. She reports that she is having diarrhea after she has gone out drinking with her friends. She states that she feels that it is the food or the alcohol or both.

The patient is alert and cooperative, appears stated age. Her lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. Her heart is regular rate and rhythm. Her abdomen is soft, non-tender. There is no masses or organomegaly. There is normal active bowel sounds.

I advised the patient to try Citrucel. It comes in a tablet. She should use it twice daily for regularity. Then I would say, if you know you're going out and will possibly have diarrhea as a result, you can go ahead and use your Bentyl 20 mg. You can do one tablet before you eat, and then repeat up to four times daily. And then on days when you feel good, you don't have to use anything. Then on Friday before you leave the house, you can take a Bentyl and put one in your pocket for later, and then you can repeat the same thing on Saturday if you want, and that will be totally fine.

I advised her that her Bentyl is the anti-spasm medication, and I would take the higher dose.

She can get a refill of that.

