summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Adult hydrocephalus.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Christopher Watson is an 86-year-old male who presents for follow-up today. He is here for his first visit since undergoing shunt surgery on 8/1/2020. He reports that he has been doing well since his surgery. He states that his walking has improved and he is able to walk to the dining room without difficulty. He notes that he still has times where he needs assistance from his daughter. He also reports that his bladder control has improved but has leveled off and he is currently wearing diapers. He denies any issues with sores from wearing the diaper or decreased or painful urination. He notes that his headaches have not bothered him at all since his surgery. He denies any pain in his belly. He states that he has been doing better and is able to have a normal conversation with someone.

MEDICAL HISTORY

Mr. Watson has a history of adult hydrocephalus with a spino catheter protocol established in 6/2020. He underwent shunt surgery on 8/1/2020. A Medtronic Strata programmable shunt in a ventricular peritoneal configuration was placed.

SOCIAL HISTORY

Mr. Watson is a pleasant and cooperative man who is able to converse easily. His daughter adds some details.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Constitutional: Denies fever or unintentional weight loss. Gastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain. Genitourinary: Reports urinary incontinence and wearing a diaper. Denies genital sores, decreased or painful output. Neurological: Denies headaches, recent falls, or hallucinations. Psychiatric: Denies depression, excessive worrying, or mood swings.

VITALS

Blood pressure is 124/80, heart rate 64, respiratory rate 18, weight 174 pounds, and pain 0 out of 10.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Neurological: Cranial nerves are intact. Pupils are equal. EOMs are intact. Face is symmetric. No dysarthria. Motor normal for bulk and strength. Coordination slow from no-finger to nose.

RESULTS

CT Scan of the Head 10/15/2020: There is a frontal horn span at the level of the foramen of Monro of 4.6 cm with a third ventricular contour that is flat with a span of 10 mm. By my reading, there is a tiny amount of blood in the right frontal region with a tiny subdermal collection. This was not noticed or noted by the radiologist who stated any extra-axial fluid collections. There is also substantial small vessel ischemic change.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Adult hydrocephalus. The patient is doing well since his shunt surgery. He has improved walking and is able to walk to the dining room. He still has times where he needs assistance from his daughter. He has also improved bladder control but has leveled off and is currently wearing diapers. He denies any issues with sores from wearing the diaper or decreased or painful urination. He notes that his headaches have not bothered him at all since his surgery. He denies any pain in his belly. He states that he has been doing better and is able to have a normal conversation with someone.

I do believe I see a tiny bit of blood fluid collection in the right parietal region on the CT scan. However, I do not believe that this is causing any symptoms and it was not noted by the radiologist when he read the CT scan. I would like to leave the shunt set for another 2-3 months before we go in changing it. If he is not having any headaches or pain and he has improved, that is all good for me.

I discussed his driving status with him today. He is still unable to drive due to his walking impairment. I told him that he could definitely enroll in a driver safety program without my recommendation. I also told him that I have occasionally had patients who surprise me by improving over a 9-12 month range, so he could definitely pass it at that point. We could continue to watch for his improvement as we go.

INSTRUCTIONS

Follow-up in 3 months.

INSTRUCTIONS

The girls up front will take care of you and get you settled for 3 months from now. }}<|eot_id|>
