summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Acne.

MEDICAL HISTORY

Patient reports a history of rosacea and perioral dermatitis.

MEDICATIONS

Doxycycline, 100 mg, oral, once daily.

SOCIAL HISTORY

Patient reports she is not breastfeeding.

MEDICATIONS

Doxycycline, 100 mg, oral, once daily.

VITALS

Fit Fitzpatrick skin type IV.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Bilateral medial cheeks, a few faint erythematous papules and mild redness around and underneath the nostrils.

ASSESSMENT

- Perioral dermatitis.
- Rosacea.

PLAN

- Medical Reasoning: The patient's symptoms have plateaued, so we will increase the doxycycline to twice daily. We will also start the tacrolimus ointment 0.03% for the perioral dermatitis. We will continue the sulfacetamide wash twice daily and the metro cream after washing her face. We will also use a moisturizer such as Vanicream or petroleum jelly as needed. We will follow up in 4 weeks.

INSTRUCTIONS

Continue using the same face wash. Continue with metro cream. Increase the use of doxycycline to twice a day. Use until the rash is gone, then use once a day for another week before stopping.

INSTRUCTIONS

- Continue doxycycline twice a day until the rash is gone, then once a day for another week before stopping.
- Once the rash stops, you can stop the tacrolimus ointment.
- Continue doxycycline another week and then stop.
- Continue to use the wash and metro cream.

INSTRUCTIONS

- Continue doxycycline another week and then stop.
- Continue to use the wash and metro cream.
- If it starts to flair again, try the tacrolimus ointment for a couple of days. If it is not better, restart the doxycycline.

INSTRUCTIONS

- If it's starting to flair again, you can try just doing the tacrolimus for a couple of days to see if that is enough. If it is not, then restart the doxycycline as well.
- If it does not get better, then we will have to figure out what to do next. }}<|eot_id|>
