ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Diabetic foot ulcer.

Medical Reasoning: The patient presents with a non-healing foot ulcer on the dorsal aspect of the lateral right foot. She has a history of type 2 diabetes. She reports she has had this wound for about a year. She states she has been trying to treat it with antibiotics and has had some improvement but it has not resolved. She reports no pain but does have decreased sensation in her right foot.

Patient Education and Counseling: The patient was advised that her diabetes is not well controlled and that she should make lifestyle changes to improve her glucose control. She was advised that she needs to follow up with her primary care physician to get her diabetes under control.

Treatment: I have ordered an ABI to evaluate the blood supply to her right foot. I also performed a debridement of the wound in the office. I prescribed Colaganase ointment and instructed her to apply it once a day and cover the wound with a sterile dressing. I advised her to wear a surgical shoe to take pressure off the wound. She was advised to follow up in 2 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>
