summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Bilateral knee pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Sophia Rodriguez is a pleasant 30-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of bilateral knee pain. The patient reports that she has had bilateral knee pain for approximately 6 months. The pain is described as deep and achy and is located behind the knee cap. She notes that the pain is worse with ambulation and climbing stairs. She denies any swelling or redness in the knees. She has tried ice and ace wrap in the past, but notes that she tries to avoid taking any medications. The patient states that she is currently not running due to the pain. She notes that she used to run 5-6 miles per day, but has not been able to run for the past 6 months.

The patient reports that the pain is worse when she gets up from a seated position. She denies any specific trauma. The pain is present after watching TV or going up and down stairs. She states that rest usually helps with the pain. She denies any numbness or tingling in her legs.

The patient is a runner and has been running 5-6 miles per day. She notes that she has not been able to run for the past 6 months. She has tried running on the treadmill during the winter.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports bilateral knee pain.
Neurological: Denies any numbness or tingling in the legs.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Vital signs: BP 120/80 mmHg, HR 72 bpm, RR 16 breaths per minute, Temp 98.6°F, SpO2 98% on room air.

MSK: Normal gait. Strength is 3/5 for abduction of legs bilaterally. The remainder of muscle strength for the lower extremities is 5/5. No overlying erythema, ecchymosis, or warmth of the skin appreciated. No effusion. Tenderness to palpation of the bilateral retinacular retinaculum. Positive patellar grind test. Unable to squat due to pain. Lachman anterior and posterior drawer and McMurray tests are negative bilaterally.

RESULTS

X-rays of the bilateral knees show no fractures or osteoarthritis.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Bilateral knee pain.

Medical Reasoning: The patient presents with bilateral knee pain. The pain is deep and achy, located behind the knee cap, and is worse with ambulation and climbing stairs. She denies any trauma. The pain is present after watching TV or going up and down stairs. She states that rest usually helps with the pain. She denies any numbness or tingling in her legs. The patient is a runner and has been running 5-6 miles per day, but has not been able to run for the past 6 months. She has tried running on the treadmill during the winter. The patient's symptoms are consistent with patellofemoral pain syndrome. This is a very common condition that we see that causes knee pain, especially in active young people. This condition has to do with the way the kneecap moves along the groove of the thigh. For the pain, she may continue taking ibuprofen or anti-inflammatory as needed to help with the pain. I am going to recommend physical therapy where they will show her a number of lower extremity exercises. This will help increase her lower extremity strength, her mobility, and correct any incorrect running mechanics that she may have. She will need to take it easy from running for now.

INSTRUCTIONS

I will be following up with the patient soon. }}<|eot_id|>
